00:02:56.577 --> 00:02:59.000
So, people are living longer.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.280
[LAUGH] Societies are getting grayer and I
know you know that.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.020
We read about it in the newspapers nearly
everyday.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.480
Some article on aging and aging societies.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.829
We hear about it on our television sets on
a regular basis on the news.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:21.730
And sometimes I worry that we hear about
it so

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:25.600
much that we've come to accept this with a
kind of a complacency.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.630
You know, you sort of say, what could be
slower and steadier, and

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:33.640
maybe drearier, than getting older, and
older, and older?

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:41.607
And I think we're thinking about this
often, outside of the historical context,

00:03:41.607 --> 00:03:48.162
a remarkable historical context, in which
this aging thing is happening.

00:03:48.162 --> 00:03:52.262
So, what I wanna do, just for a minute, is
to kinda zoom out, and

00:03:52.262 --> 00:03:55.690
consider the point in history, where we
are living.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:02.110
Let me start by saying that more years
were added

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:09.850
to average life expectancy in the 20th
century than all years added

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:15.690
across all prior millenia of human
evolution combined.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.930
In a blink of an eye, in historical terms,

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:24.690
we nearly doubled the length of the lives
that we are living.

00:04:26.755 --> 00:04:30.440
17th century philosopher Thomas Hobbs had
it right,

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.160
he described life as nasty, brutish and

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.680
short [LAUGH] and throughout most of human
evolution, that's exactly what it was.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.530
We don't know what life expectancy was as
we were or

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.730
our ancestors were evolving on, on the
African savannah.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.646
Estimates go from 18 to about 20,

00:04:51.646 --> 00:04:56.339
[LAUGH] but there's considerable agreement
that life expectancy was short.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:03.730
Now evolution acted on age on life
expectancy in these early years.

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:07.890
It acted in a way that evolution acts, a
snail like pace.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.770
And the average length of life got a
little bit longer, little bit longer,

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:15.628
little bit longer.

00:05:15.628 --> 00:05:21.158
By the mid-1800's life expectancy in the
United States

00:05:21.158 --> 00:05:27.661
had reached the mid-30's and by 1900 life
expectancy was 47.

00:05:27.661 --> 00:05:36.880
At the end of that same century life
expectancy was 77 and today, it's 79.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.180
And still going up.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.010
This change is not finished with us yet.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:46.000
In recent years, three months are being
added to

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.920
average life expectancy at 65 every year.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.464
So every four years we're getting an
additional year of life at 65.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.500
Now, across that very same period that

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.734
life expectancy was going up so
dramatically.

00:06:04.734 --> 00:06:11.990
Fertility rates drop precipitously, by
half.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:17.800
So in 1900 the average number of children
born to an American woman was 4.2.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.010
Like the decimal points.

00:06:20.996 --> 00:06:27.855
And by the end of the century that number
went to 2.1.

00:06:27.855 --> 00:06:34.296
And the combination of increased life
expectancy, people living longer,

00:06:34.296 --> 00:06:38.933
and having few children resulted in aging
societies.

00:06:38.933 --> 00:06:44.040
So we could have had longer lives, without
aging societies.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:49.690
We would have much larger populations, but
these two phenomena together,

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:54.115
reorganized, redistributed, age, in the
population.

00:06:54.115 --> 00:07:01.455
So, in, in 1900, 4% of the population in
this country was over 65.

00:07:01.455 --> 00:07:05.028
Today 14% is over 65.

00:07:05.028 --> 00:07:09.037
And I have to say, I had to update that
percentage from last year which was

00:07:09.037 --> 00:07:13.601
13% [LAUGH] so these numbers just keep
bumping up, you've gotta stay current.

00:07:13.601 --> 00:07:21.890
14% today, and it will go to 20% by 2030,
20% of the population will be over 65.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:24.240
Now we remain the young kid on the block,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.488
compared to other developed nations that
are already much older.

00:07:27.488 --> 00:07:32.310
In Japan, 16% of the population today is
over 65,

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:38.070
in Japan, 20% of the population is over 65
today and

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:44.028
by 2030 that number will increase to 28%.

00:07:44.028 --> 00:07:51.410
Now these numbers are game changers for
really every aspect of life as we know it.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:55.960
education, work, retirement, families,
politics,

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:01.710
all are going to change as a function of
this shift from being young populations,

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.910
young societies, to aging populations.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:12.499
Now just to be clear as to so, what, what
is happening here,

00:08:12.499 --> 00:08:19.333
let me distinguish an increase in life
expectancy from an increase in life span.

00:08:19.333 --> 00:08:23.191
Often we hear about aging societies and
increases in life span,

00:08:23.191 --> 00:08:26.774
potential increases in life span, all
bundled together.

00:08:26.774 --> 00:08:29.010
But these are very different phenomena.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:31.454
And to the best of our knowledge,

00:08:31.454 --> 00:08:37.200
life span has not increased across the
years of human evolution.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.540
What changed was life expectancy.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:42.810
The average length of life, but not the
capacity.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:47.460
Now, ever since I've been in this field,
there have been voices

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:52.659
talking about the possibility of removing
the cap on human mortality.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.330
In fact, humans have been searching for

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.745
the Fountain of Youth ever since we
realized we were mortal.

00:08:57.745 --> 00:09:01.602
[LAUGH] We used to sail off in ships and
now we turn to laboratories, but

00:09:01.602 --> 00:09:06.030
there's still a lot of people seeing if
they can solve that conundrum.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:10.453
Death.
[LAUGH] Generally those voices have come

00:09:10.453 --> 00:09:13.287
from the scientific fringe.

00:09:13.287 --> 00:09:19.627
But you know, in these parts when Google
gets interested [LAUGH] we all take note.

00:09:19.627 --> 00:09:24.304
And Google has now as many of you know
formed a new company called Calico and

00:09:24.304 --> 00:09:27.935
hired Art Levinson the former CEO of
Genentech to run it.

00:09:27.935 --> 00:09:33.501
And let me say also that there are a lot
of very distinguished biologists

00:09:33.501 --> 00:09:38.787
who are thinking they might be able to
find a way to increase life span

00:09:38.787 --> 00:09:44.940
through some sorts of scientific and
medical advances in the 21st century.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:51.920
Now, these remain promises at this point
in time or guesses, hypothesis.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.330
It has not happened yet.

00:09:54.508 --> 00:10:00.545
But increasingly, people are beginning to
consider the possibility

00:10:00.545 --> 00:10:07.504
that life expectancy could actually
increase dramatically, substantially,

00:10:07.504 --> 00:10:13.147
beyond the current ages, because of some
potential of science.

00:10:13.147 --> 00:10:18.409
Now when we think about lifespan, how long
it's possible for an organism to live and

00:10:18.409 --> 00:10:22.990
humans we have to acknowledge that this
remains a theoretical concept.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.060
We don't know what lifespan is in humans.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.594
We know what it is in earthworms, we know
what it is in gypsophila.

00:10:28.594 --> 00:10:31.981
Where we can observe, you know, in petri
dishes and so

00:10:31.981 --> 00:10:34.851
on, their, their, their lifespans, but,

00:10:34.851 --> 00:10:39.807
you know, this would, this, this is just
a, a, a, a concept in humans.

00:10:39.807 --> 00:10:40.541
We don't know.

00:10:40.541 --> 00:10:46.511
It would mean, what would happen, how long
would life be if we found cures for

00:10:46.511 --> 00:10:51.356
all of the diseases, we removed all of the
environmental toxins,

00:10:51.356 --> 00:10:54.904
we have reduced accidental deaths to near
zero,

00:10:54.904 --> 00:10:59.316
we would be able to see what life's span
would look like, but

00:10:59.316 --> 00:11:05.140
when guesses are being bandied about, we
know that 120 is possible.

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:10.487
122 is possible because the first woman,

00:11:10.487 --> 00:11:15.707
ever to have been documented [LAUGH] as
the longest

00:11:15.707 --> 00:11:21.716
lived person ever died at the age of 122
in 1997.

00:11:21.716 --> 00:11:25.135
Jeanne Loise Calment was a French woman
who lived in the French city of Arles.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:32.010
And I like to tell people about her
because she's pretty inspirational.

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.070
I mean this is a woman who to very,

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:41.600
very advanced ages, to very shortly before
her death was sharp, engaged,

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.735
funny, she made a rap album when she was
in her 90's.

00:11:46.735 --> 00:11:51.160
[LAUGH] She played herself in the movie
Vincent and

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:53.110
Me which was about Vincent van Gogh.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.830
She had actually met him, she was the only
person on the planet who had

00:11:55.830 --> 00:12:00.635
actually known him, [LAUGH] and that made
her the oldest actress of all time.

00:12:00.635 --> 00:12:05.750
[LAUGH] She was interviewed a lot as you
might imagine and and

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.670
she was very funny, but one, one,

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:13.600
one journalist had posed the question,
what sort of a future do you envision?

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:17.782
And Calment thought about it and said, a
very short one.

00:12:17.782 --> 00:12:20.441
[LAUGH].

00:12:20.441 --> 00:12:22.677
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:12:22.677 --> 00:12:27.528
But my favorite Calment story hands down
concerns a property deal that she

00:12:27.528 --> 00:12:29.649
made at the ripe old age of 90.

00:12:31.225 --> 00:12:33.688
Kalment owned her own home as I mentioned
in the French city of Arles.

00:12:33.688 --> 00:12:35.523
And it was a family home.

00:12:35.523 --> 00:12:37.058
She'd lived in it most of her life.

00:12:37.058 --> 00:12:41.697
And she had every intention of living out
her life in this home.

00:12:41.697 --> 00:12:42.830
But there was this lawyer.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:44.432
A young guy, he was 47 years old.

00:12:44.432 --> 00:12:47.068
He really wanted to buy the house.

00:12:47.068 --> 00:12:50.060
And he had made her offers over the years.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:51.380
And she'd turned them all down.

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:56.130
And he would show up and he'd make her a
better offer, and she'd turn him down, and

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:57.970
that was the way things went for a long
time, and

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:01.460
then one day he shows up on her doorstep
and he says I've got a deal for you.

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:05.637
I will pay you $400 a month for

00:13:05.637 --> 00:13:10.791
the rest of your life if you will deed the
house to me on your death.

00:13:10.791 --> 00:13:11.692
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:13:11.692 --> 00:13:13.962
[LAUGH]

00:13:13.962 --> 00:13:22.136
She thinks about it.

00:13:22.136 --> 00:13:24.172
She says okay.

00:13:24.172 --> 00:13:31.405
And they draw up a contract and over the
next 30 years.

00:13:31.405 --> 00:13:35.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:40.315
&gt;&gt; This fellow paid her more than three
times the value of her home.

00:13:40.315 --> 00:13:44.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:46.895
&gt;&gt; She outlived him by two years [LAUGH].

00:13:46.895 --> 00:13:52.399
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].
[LAUGH].

00:13:52.399 --> 00:13:55.234
&gt;&gt; He died at 77.

00:13:55.234 --> 00:13:57.505
They'd become friends over the years,
though.

00:13:57.505 --> 00:14:00.155
And they were overheard chatting at, at,
at,

00:14:00.155 --> 00:14:04.030
at her, 120th birthday party, shortly
before he died.

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:06.581
And, [LAUGH] she had turned to him at one
point, and

00:14:06.581 --> 00:14:08.482
said, you know, we all make bad deals.

00:14:08.482 --> 00:14:12.720
[LAUGH].

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:19.060
So that's what 122 can look like.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:23.998
But an increase in life span, increasingly
more people living to these very, very,

00:14:23.998 --> 00:14:27.301
very advanced ages had nothing to do with
these changes.

00:14:27.301 --> 00:14:31.238
The changes that we are living through
today.

00:14:31.238 --> 00:14:38.140
These changes reflect life expectancy
changes.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:42.050
Differences in the odds of making it to
old age,

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:45.356
differences in the average length of life.

00:14:45.356 --> 00:14:49.593
And essentially what has happened, is that
these population pyramids,

00:14:49.593 --> 00:14:53.984
that we learned about in school, do you
remember the population pyramids?

00:14:53.984 --> 00:14:56.759
Cuz when I lecture younger people today
they don't.

00:14:56.759 --> 00:14:57.456
You're very young.

00:14:57.456 --> 00:15:00.938
[LAUGH] So population pyramids,.

00:15:00.938 --> 00:15:06.570
Represented the, the populations in every
country on the globe as recently as 1950.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:09.480
And they also, by the way, represent most
species.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:16.730
Basically, you have many at the bottom,
young ones born in a population and

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:18.320
then the distribution has winnowed to a
tiny peak at the top.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:23.130
That's the way populations have
historically been shaped.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:29.620
And what has been happening over the years
is that these pyramids in the developed

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:35.386
world are being reshaped into rectangles.

00:15:35.386 --> 00:15:40.770
And if you're the kind

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:43.694
of person who can get chills from
population statistics [LAUGH] my people.

00:15:43.694 --> 00:15:48.570
[LAUGH] These are the ones that should do
it because what this means is that for

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:54.470
the first time in history, the vast
majority of babies born

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:59.650
in the developed world are having the
opportunity to grow old.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:05.140
And this is an astounding cultural
achievement.

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:10.920
We dramatically reduced premature death,
essentially so

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:14.710
that now most people can grow old.

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:17.716
How'd we do this?

00:16:17.716 --> 00:16:23.440
[LAUGH] well, the short answer is science
and technology.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:27.460
This remarkable increase in life
expectancy is really a product of culture.

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:30.520
And by culture I'm referring to the
crucible that holds science and

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:36.520
technology and large scale behavioral
practices and, and social norms.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:41.080
We applied science and technology and we
changed the way we

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:46.070
live in order to improve the health and
well-being of the population.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:51.090
Our ancestors in the 20th century
discovered the causes of diseases and

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.260
how they were spread.

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:56.940
And then they instituted grand public
health programs to

00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:00.610
vaccinate children against diseases that
they would never have to suffer.

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:03.240
They didn't stop there.

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:08.840
They pasteurized milk, purified the
waterways, and

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:13.120
these changes had magnificent influences
on health.

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:20.110
They also implemented the systematic
disposal of waste, garbage collection

00:17:20.110 --> 00:17:25.930
which again significantly, substantially
reduced the spread of contagious disease.

00:17:25.930 --> 00:17:30.120
And some historians write that you have
your garbage collectors to thank as much

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:33.270
as your physicians for this increase in
life expectancy.

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:38.669
We didn't stop there with science and
technology.

00:17:38.669 --> 00:17:42.140
As fertility rates fell we came to invest
more in children.

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:46.090
We had fewer of them and we said we needed
to make bigger investments and

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:52.050
we established public education in every
state in this great nations so

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:58.920
that all children could learn how to read
and write, not just the privileged few.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:02.490
And today, at some point in adulthood,

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:08.110
education becomes a better predictor of
life expectancy than age.

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:12.189
A stunning, stunning development.

00:18:12.189 --> 00:18:17.261
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:18:17.261 --> 00:18:18.410
So here we are.

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:23.780
Living in a point in history where four,

00:18:23.780 --> 00:18:28.670
five and conceivably six generations could
be alive at the same time.

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:33.130
You know, contrast this with a 100 years
ago.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:40.280
A 20-year-old in 1900 was less likely

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:45.880
to have a living mother than a 20-year-old
today is of having a living grandmother.

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:53.284
And a demographer at Berkeley predicts
that by 2030, the majority of children

00:18:53.284 --> 00:18:58.804
in the United States will not only have
two living biological parents but

00:18:58.804 --> 00:19:03.441
a full compliment of grand parents and
great grand parents.

00:19:03.441 --> 00:19:08.011
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:19:08.011 --> 00:19:12.749
We need to make this the best thing that
ever happened to children.

00:19:12.749 --> 00:19:21.325
We need to turn this demographic change
into a resource.

00:19:21.325 --> 00:19:23.415
But, we can not be doing that yet as a
society.

00:19:23.415 --> 00:19:28.928
Instead, when we think about aging
societies,

00:19:28.928 --> 00:19:35.929
these days, mostly, we're hearing about
fear, concern.

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:42.550
About older people absorbing all of the
resources in society and

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:44.320
leaving the young ones on their own.

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:48.800
We're hearing about disorders like
osteoporosis, osteopenia,

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:52.890
arthritis, as limiting people's functional
ability.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:56.580
Some of you will have read Zeke Emmanuel's
article in the Atlantic recently which is

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:59.561
titled, I hope I die by 75.

00:19:59.561 --> 00:20:05.360
[LAUGH] Where he's saying he doesn't want
to live a disabled life.

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:09.330
We're hearing that people haven't saved
enough for

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:14.340
retirement and so we're going to see
poverty rates increase.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:19.980
We're seeing populations and work forces
in many old, rich countries shrink,

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:24.810
while we see the populations in young,
poor countries expand.

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:30.940
Now these are, these are real challenges
that need to be addressed.

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:37.270
As I said, I think they really reflect the
mismatch between the length of

00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:41.440
lives that we're living and the culture
that guides us through life.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:47.010
And the Stanford Center on Longevity was
founded in order to

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:52.309
redesign culture so that it supports
people all the way through.

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:57.050
I mean essentially, we think that the
culture we live in today, which evolved

00:20:57.050 --> 00:21:00.380
around lives half as long, does a pretty
good job of supporting people up to 50.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:04.580
And then it kinda stops.

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:09.530
Now, a lot of people do very well after
50, but they're far fewer guide points and

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:14.890
norms and societal resources that, that
are helping people structure their lives.

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:16.690
And humans are creatures of culture.

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:21.290
We listen to culture to tell us when to
get an eduction, when to marry,

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:22.840
when to have children, when to retire.

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:29.050
And we need to change that mismatch and we
need to do it with considerable speed.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:43.010
As we learn about aging, we're finding
that the malleability, the plasticity,

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:49.930
the potential for people to age well is
greater than ever previously imagined.

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:55.014
Science is beginning to catch up with
this, but

00:21:55.014 --> 00:22:00.817
I made this slide to try to summarized all
the research on aging in a single image.

00:22:00.817 --> 00:22:05.641
[LAUGH] And I think this really is the
model that's guiding most

00:22:05.641 --> 00:22:11.595
research on aging, you know, we say, well,
there, here's a variable?

00:22:11.595 --> 00:22:15.170
I'll compare younger people to older
people, and older people won't do so well.

00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:21.050
And that's not a bad question to ask,
anybody here, you know,

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:26.430
over the age of 50 knows that there's a
lot not to like about aging these days.

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:32.550
So, we do want to seek knowledge about the
problems and a better understanding.

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:35.570
But if you only ask questions about what
goes wrong,

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:38.510
you generate a literature about what goes
wrong,

00:22:39.530 --> 00:22:44.410
and we need to begin to ask questions
about what may go right.

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:47.750
What may facilitate and help people's
aging.

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:50.390
And the more that we learn,

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:53.750
the more empirical data that are
collected, the more we see that

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:58.509
this model is not the typical model that
represents most aging trajectories.

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:04.619
So let me tell you just a very little bit
about a range of

00:23:04.619 --> 00:23:09.815
different topics that have been studied to
point out the, the, the,

00:23:09.815 --> 00:23:14.532
the, these, the nuances that we are
beginning to identify.

00:23:14.532 --> 00:23:21.896
We have learned over the last 50 years
that processing speed, learning,

00:23:21.896 --> 00:23:28.672
taking in new novel situations and
information, tends to decline steadily.

00:23:28.672 --> 00:23:33.176
And it begins at the ripe old age of 23.

00:23:33.176 --> 00:23:37.314
[LAUGH] And we see this kind of steady
downward slope.

00:23:37.314 --> 00:23:42.100
That's certainly been the traditional way
of characterizing learning, and

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:43.487
the ability to learn.

00:23:43.487 --> 00:23:46.199
It's not that people can't learn, as they
get older, but

00:23:46.199 --> 00:23:48.320
it's a little more degraded.

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:50.160
A little slower, takes a little bit more
time.

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:55.990
This has been the focus of most of
research on cognitive aging.

00:23:55.990 --> 00:24:02.780
But just as sure, knowledge across the
same years is going up.

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:06.760
So new learning may take a little bit
longer, but people have lived longer and

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:08.180
they have more time to learn.

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:10.280
And so we see knowledge trajectories go
up.

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:18.020
And I want to point out that this line has
to be a great underestimate of knowledge,

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:22.110
because it's given or tapped by test of
general cultural knowledge,

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:25.880
by vocabulary, or, knowledge, answers to
questions, you know,

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:28.110
like what is the capital of Afghanist-,

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:28.870
in Afghanistan.

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:31.830
You know, I mean, those are the kinds of
questions that people are asking.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:35.960
They're not being asked questions about
expertise in the areas in which

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:37.914
they have developed great depth of
knowledge.

00:24:37.914 --> 00:24:45.380
So we're underestimating this knowledge,
this knowledge potential, undoubtedly.

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:52.690
And in very recent years, we've begun even
to challenge the red line.

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:55.360
That were seeing this steady decline.

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:56.710
And here's why.

00:24:56.710 --> 00:25:02.510
Because we've been running studies long
enough now to have longitudinal databases

00:25:02.510 --> 00:25:08.580
on individuals who have been tested
repeatedly throughout mid to late life.

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:11.050
And they've been studied long enough to
know that some people have gone

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:15.720
on to develop Alzheimer's disease.

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:20.860
And these are data from David Bennett's
laboratory, from

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:21.700
a longitudinal study he ran.

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:25.720
And he's been measuring this processing
speed thing that goes down so gradually.

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:29.233
And he removes people who got Alzheimer's
disease and

00:25:29.233 --> 00:25:34.131
he recalculates what that decline looks
like, and looks like the red line.

00:25:34.131 --> 00:25:35.479
In the blue line, those people,

00:25:35.479 --> 00:25:38.995
these are not people who have symptoms of
Alzheimer's disease, any sort of sign, but

00:25:38.995 --> 00:25:41.038
they're people who are gonna go on to
develop it.

00:25:41.038 --> 00:25:44.341
And they're showing a steep decline, but

00:25:44.341 --> 00:25:50.040
a much more modest decline in those
individuals who will remain healthy.

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:53.975
And if there's any good news about
Alzheimer's disease and there is not much,

00:25:53.975 --> 00:26:00.140
it's that the majority of people won't
ever suffer from the disease,

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:04.320
even into the 90s, 60 some odd percent of
people will not get Alzheimer's disease.

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:08.699
So even though the rates go up it's not
like it's inevitable.

00:26:08.699 --> 00:26:10.435
So you pull those two things apart.

00:26:10.435 --> 00:26:15.703
My point is, it's possible that we come up
with these slopes, these pretty slopes,

00:26:15.703 --> 00:26:21.390
because we've been compounding, inflating
disease with normal cognitive aging.

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:25.020
And it might help us understand why, it
sure seems like there a lot,

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:28.810
an awful lot of older people who are very
quick on the uptake.

00:26:28.810 --> 00:26:30.530
They likely are, the majority.

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:34.470
So, we're beginning to have to even
rethink some of

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:37.299
these long held beliefs about cognition
and aging.

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:43.020
Well, there's a really good story about
emotion and aging.

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:45.480
It looks like emotional life improves with
age.

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:52.140
Here are recent findings from a Gallup
Poll conducted in 2011

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:57.719
comparing people from 18 to 85, and their
responses on a question.

00:26:58.930 --> 00:27:01.460
How much stress did you experience
yesterday?

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:02.910
These are yesterday interviews.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:10.570
And you see precipitous drop in older
people as compared to younger.

00:27:10.570 --> 00:27:11.230
How much worry?

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:13.980
How much anger?

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:19.360
And across all different negative emotions
you see reductions

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:24.390
the older people are, in their responses
to that question.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:28.300
Now, we worried in my research group,
we've done some work like this too.

00:27:28.300 --> 00:27:29.246
This is called a cross-sectional study.

00:27:29.246 --> 00:27:32.890
We're comparing people at one single point
in time, of different ages.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:36.480
And you always worry with findings like
that is that it may not have

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:38.590
something to do with aging so much.

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:41.760
It might be that you're comparing
different generations.

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:45.607
So maybe the greatest generation is and
always was the greatest generation,

00:27:45.607 --> 00:27:49.218
you know, and they're doing fine but it
doesn't mean necessarily that

00:27:49.218 --> 00:27:52.736
younger people today can anticipate better
emotional functioning.

00:27:52.736 --> 00:27:57.972
But over a decade ago my group began a
longitudinal study with an approach we

00:27:57.972 --> 00:27:59.977
call experience sampling.

00:27:59.977 --> 00:28:03.644
People carry a pager and five times a day
we page them at random times and they tell

00:28:03.644 --> 00:28:07.245
us the degree to which their feet, feeling
each of 19 different emotions.

00:28:07.245 --> 00:28:11.065
[LAUGH] And we chart this over the years.

00:28:11.065 --> 00:28:17.290
[LAUGH] And what we see, is something that
looks very much like that.

00:28:17.290 --> 00:28:21.990
Within individuals, it looks like
emotional lives improved.

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:25.970
Again, this is a great resource for
societies.

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:28.116
Having emotionally stable,

00:28:28.116 --> 00:28:33.420
relatively positively balanced, emotional
lives, it's terrific.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:36.630
You know, you think of the kinds of
challenges that we face at this point in

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:40.940
history and having some of those folks in
the population is a very good thing.

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:45.280
Well, what about health?

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:47.740
What about physical health?

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:52.538
When demographers first realized just how
big these changes were,

00:28:52.538 --> 00:28:54.790
they were terrified.

00:28:54.790 --> 00:28:57.800
They were worried that we'd created a
monster of sorts.

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:02.790
That is, people were going to live longer
but they would be infirm longer.

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:06.280
And the good news is, that doesn't seem to
have happened.

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:11.180
The, most of the added years in adulthood
are healthy years.

00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:18.500
This is a slide from a colleague of mine,
John Shoven, who is an economist here

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:24.170
at Stanford and has argued that we've been
measuring age the wrong way.

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:29.770
We've been measuring it as how many years
since you were born.

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:32.310
And another way to think about age is how
many years do you have left.

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:38.112
And if we think about it that way, well,
for

00:29:38.112 --> 00:29:42.479
one he says that social security and the
medicare problems are solved.

00:29:42.479 --> 00:29:46.450
So, so, so we, we, we, could readjust how,
how, how we measure age.

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:47.830
How we think about life.

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:55.271
And do you know how people say these days
that 60 is the new 40?

00:29:55.271 --> 00:29:58.309
Well, here's the thing.

00:29:58.309 --> 00:30:01.494
65 really is the new 59.

00:30:01.494 --> 00:30:03.630
[LAUGH] Here are these data.

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:06.445
So, John says, let's not decide-

00:30:06.445 --> 00:30:10.001
Let, let's not decide if people are old or
not based on how long they've,

00:30:10.001 --> 00:30:14.350
it's been since they were born, let's
think about what the mortality risk is.

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:16.350
So at what age do you reach

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:22.830
the odds of dying are one in a 100, two in
a 100, four in a 100.

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:26.752
And basically what John says is, when your
mortality risk hits 2% that is

00:30:26.752 --> 00:30:30.563
you've got a 2% chance of dying in the
next year, we'll call you old.

00:30:30.563 --> 00:30:34.231
[LAUGH] But what you see is that over
historical time-

00:30:34.231 --> 00:30:38.401
Now keep in mind this is not age down here
on the x axis this time,

00:30:38.401 --> 00:30:40.141
this is historical time.

00:30:40.141 --> 00:30:46.085
And people are not reaching that age until
increasingly later in life.

00:30:46.085 --> 00:30:51.236
It looks like we've pushed out the health,
poor health, or

00:30:51.236 --> 00:30:55.082
health problems, farther into the future.

00:30:55.082 --> 00:30:59.192
Now we have a lot more work to do to
compress morbidity at the end.

00:30:59.192 --> 00:31:02.620
But it is not the case that the added
years in

00:31:02.620 --> 00:31:06.142
adulthood are added years of illness.

00:31:06.142 --> 00:31:09.838
We worry about the next generations coming
up.

00:31:09.838 --> 00:31:14.591
We worry about the boomers because we're
seeing more obesity and

00:31:14.591 --> 00:31:17.615
sedentary behavior, more chronic diseases.

00:31:17.615 --> 00:31:20.602
There're not guarantees that this is gonna
continue, but

00:31:20.602 --> 00:31:23.766
over the last 50 years, you know, if you
take the long view,

00:31:23.766 --> 00:31:26.530
there's no question that health has been
improving.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:32.143
Education matters and it matters a lot.

00:31:32.143 --> 00:31:38.963
[LAUGH] There's good news and bad news in
this slide but let me walk you through it.

00:31:38.963 --> 00:31:43.195
What you're seeing on the y axis here is
the proportion of

00:31:43.195 --> 00:31:48.185
people at different ages who have physical
limitations.

00:31:48.185 --> 00:31:52.527
Functional limitations, that is, they
can't do things in life.

00:31:52.527 --> 00:31:57.248
So we can talk about health in a lot of
different ways.

00:31:57.248 --> 00:32:00.546
We could talk about it in terms of
mortality risk, as John Shoven is doing.

00:32:00.546 --> 00:32:02.868
We could say how many diseases or
diagnoses do you have?

00:32:02.868 --> 00:32:05.479
Or we can say, how well can you live your
life?

00:32:06.850 --> 00:32:11.410
So what you see represented here are the
proportion of people at different ages,

00:32:11.410 --> 00:32:16.290
who say yes to all the following four
questions.

00:32:16.290 --> 00:32:21.410
Things like can you go up a couple flights
of stairs without any problem?

00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:22.560
Can you pay your own bills?

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:24.180
You live independently in your own house?

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:25.600
You know, basically people are saying,

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:29.180
I don't have any problem in living my own
life, on my own terms.

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:33.024
So that's what you're seeing charted here,
by age.

00:32:33.024 --> 00:32:38.886
You're also seeing it in different colors,
illustrated by education level.

00:32:38.886 --> 00:32:42.566
And this is a really important story.

00:32:42.566 --> 00:32:44.374
What you see is that in the 30s,

00:32:44.374 --> 00:32:48.580
you don't see a lot of difference across
these different education groups.

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:51.465
Basically everybody's relatively healthy,

00:32:51.465 --> 00:32:54.500
regardless of education level in their
30's.

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:58.302
And people start to get sick again 85 and
beyond, and

00:32:58.302 --> 00:33:01.956
we think that's because people do get sick
and die.

00:33:01.956 --> 00:33:04.528
So far, 100%.

00:33:04.528 --> 00:33:09.980
[LAUGH] So, what you see in middle though,
is this big dif, distribution.

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:14.497
And what you're seeing here, if you look
at the red line, people with,

00:33:14.497 --> 00:33:17.175
with high levels of education in society.

00:33:17.175 --> 00:33:22.334
Their functional health, the majority of
people say 80% of

00:33:22.334 --> 00:33:26.326
people in their 70s, 80% are doing just
fine!

00:33:26.326 --> 00:33:27.855
This is huge.

00:33:27.855 --> 00:33:33.627
This means that it's possible to grow old
well, in terms of physical health.

00:33:33.627 --> 00:33:37.773
The bad news in the slide [LAUGH] is that
the majority of people in

00:33:37.773 --> 00:33:41.300
this country don't have high levels of
education.

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:49.440
So, it's sobering in some ways to see this
steady decline in functional ability,

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:51.730
and people with less than a high school
education, or

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:54.730
little education, because this is the
country we're living in.

00:33:55.930 --> 00:33:59.875
But it, it shows us how important
education is, and

00:33:59.875 --> 00:34:04.010
should inspire us to see that ageing well
is possible.

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:10.649
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:34:10.649 --> 00:34:13.535
We believe that the Center on Longevity
that,

00:34:13.535 --> 00:34:18.197
to the extent that the majority of people
arrive at old age mentally sharp,

00:34:18.197 --> 00:34:22.360
physically fit, and financially secure
societies will thrive.

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:22.960
And that is our aim.

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:26.280
So we have three research divisions.

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:29.290
One on mind, one on mobility, one on
financial security.

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:32.530
And we are doing our best to apply the
best of science and

00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:38.680
technology, so that we can improve
functioning in those three domains.

00:34:38.680 --> 00:34:40.890
We think of them as three legs of a stool.

00:34:40.890 --> 00:34:43.670
And if any one of them is missing, we're
in trouble.

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:48.820
But if we can get those, those three legs
giving us steady support,

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:50.910
aging societies can thrive.

00:34:50.910 --> 00:34:56.310
And you know, the really good news today
is that we're living at a time in history,

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:58.620
as this life expectancy's going up,

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:03.230
we're living at a time when the potentials
of science are truly breathtaking.

00:35:04.500 --> 00:35:05.740
I mean, just last week,

00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:11.820
Rudy Tanzi at Harvard, reported in an
article published in Nature,

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:17.750
that he had developed a way to use stem
cells to grow into human brain cells.

00:35:17.750 --> 00:35:22.180
And he could then implant the DNA
associated with Alzheimer's disease and

00:35:22.180 --> 00:35:25.790
watch the disease unfold with great speed.

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:32.260
What this means is that we'll be able to
have drug trials occur far faster

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:35.280
than they have been in recent years.

00:35:35.280 --> 00:35:38.629
It's one sign of a kind of hope that we'll
find a cure for

00:35:38.629 --> 00:35:43.406
some of these pernicious diseases that rob
so many people of their own quality of

00:35:43.406 --> 00:35:46.847
life, along with the quality of life of
their families.

00:35:46.847 --> 00:35:48.428
And technology?

00:35:48.428 --> 00:35:50.468
Well here we are in Silicon Valley,

00:35:50.468 --> 00:35:54.740
we know the potentials of technology are
extraordinary.

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:59.350
I'm gonna give you one example of a
technological advance that

00:35:59.350 --> 00:36:03.110
was developed by a biomechanical engineer
here at Stanford and

00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:04.530
is affiliated with our center.

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:06.430
His name is Tom Andriacki.

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:08.389
He's been studying Gate for many years.

00:36:09.590 --> 00:36:15.770
And what he's observed is that people,
humans tend to walk in a particular way,

00:36:15.770 --> 00:36:19.910
and what happens is we put our weight
primarily on the medial knee and

00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:22.970
that's where most people develop
osteoarthritis.

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:24.700
Age related osteoarthritis.

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:29.250
And over the years, after he'd studied
this and

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:34.180
studied this he thought, you know, we
should be able to shift this gait.

00:36:34.180 --> 00:36:35.480
And he and his students and

00:36:35.480 --> 00:36:39.790
colleagues began working on what is called
a variable stiffness sole.

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:46.773
And the sole can be put into virtually any
shoe, and it shifts the weight of your

00:36:46.773 --> 00:36:52.528
gait away from the medial knee, in a way
that is subjectively indistinguishable.

00:36:52.528 --> 00:36:55.570
I mean, you can't tell that it's doing any
of this.

00:36:55.570 --> 00:37:01.170
And in Tom's work, he's shown that this
reduces the development of osteoarthritis,

00:37:01.170 --> 00:37:05.190
and also relieves pain in the joint
immediately.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:08.410
So Tom came over and talked to us at the
center and,

00:37:08.410 --> 00:37:12.294
you know, we said things to him like,
that's not pretty, that shoe.

00:37:12.294 --> 00:37:13.774
[LAUGH] And

00:37:13.774 --> 00:37:18.525
Jane Fonda who is on our advisory council,
looked at the shoe and she went, no.

00:37:18.525 --> 00:37:21.853
[LAUGH] And she starts talking to Stuart
Weismann.

00:37:21.853 --> 00:37:23.921
The women in this room know who Stuart
Weismann is.

00:37:23.921 --> 00:37:27.739
And says, you know, we gotta get prettier
shoes and we're still working on

00:37:27.739 --> 00:37:31.199
really pretty ones, but at least we've got
one that looks better and

00:37:31.199 --> 00:37:34.140
is available today in the Walking
Company's store.

00:37:34.140 --> 00:37:35.260
So you can get this shoe.

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:38.132
It's referred to as the Standford shoe.

00:37:38.132 --> 00:37:40.857
Or the ABEO shoe.

00:37:40.857 --> 00:37:46.437
But this is the kind of product that we
believe can come from technological

00:37:46.437 --> 00:37:51.305
developments and advances and really
improve quality of life.

00:37:51.305 --> 00:37:53.614
We think of this as just one example and

00:37:53.614 --> 00:37:57.220
I offer it to you as the kind of example
that if we can bring and

00:37:57.220 --> 00:38:01.047
distribute these kinds of products into
the culture broadly,

00:38:01.047 --> 00:38:06.630
just like electricity, and refrigeration,
and, and the systematic disposal of waste.

00:38:06.630 --> 00:38:10.810
Those kinds of changes that our ancestors
made 100 years ago,

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:16.000
then we can use this gift of longer years
really to have more

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:21.180
time to chase our dreams and spend with
our loved ones and, and pursue our goals.

00:38:21.180 --> 00:38:24.893
In fact, we think that these added years
of life may for

00:38:24.893 --> 00:38:29.591
the first time give us the opportunity to
redesign life altogether.

00:38:29.591 --> 00:38:33.141
This is the model of life that we live
today.

00:38:33.141 --> 00:38:37.046
And it's a model of life parsed by age,
where you get your education early life,

00:38:37.046 --> 00:38:40.895
you work really, you work like a dog in
the middle of life basically, you know,

00:38:40.895 --> 00:38:44.009
and take care of kids and often older
relatives at the same time.

00:38:44.009 --> 00:38:47.037
And then eventually, you retire from work
and you get a little leisure.

00:38:47.037 --> 00:38:48.185
That's our model today.

00:38:48.185 --> 00:38:49.580
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:52.724
And it works for 50.

00:38:52.724 --> 00:38:58.234
[LAUGH] If life expectancy is 50 years,
it's not a bad model.

00:38:58.234 --> 00:39:01.985
But we've now got the opportunity to have
very different models.

00:39:01.985 --> 00:39:04.028
People are gonna work longer.

00:39:04.028 --> 00:39:05.850
You think we'll be stretching out work.

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:10.350
We could also stretch out leisure, so you
don't have to wait to 65 and

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:12.310
retirement in order to get a break.

00:39:12.310 --> 00:39:17.370
We can have education begin in early
adulthood, and

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:20.677
continue all the way through.

00:39:20.677 --> 00:39:25.853
Phil Pizzo, who many of you know as the
Dean of Stanford Medical School.

00:39:25.853 --> 00:39:31.107
When he stepped down a, a year ago, came
to the Center on Longevity, and

00:39:31.107 --> 00:39:36.535
he embarked on a new project called the
Distinguished Careers Institute,

00:39:36.535 --> 00:39:40.929
where he is bringing back a class of
people from mid career and

00:39:40.929 --> 00:39:45.720
late career back to Stanford for a year,
as students.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:46.860
That’s the kind of model.

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:51.460
A model of education where universities
have revolving doors, and

00:39:51.460 --> 00:39:54.550
people do come early and then they come
back, and they leave and

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:56.510
they come back, and they leave and they
come back.

00:39:56.510 --> 00:39:59.945
These are the kinds of new models that
will change the culture.

00:39:59.945 --> 00:40:04.509
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:40:04.509 --> 00:40:10.568
So thank you for your interest in, in
longevity.

00:40:10.568 --> 00:40:14.596
I am going to stop and turn things over to
my colleague Ken Smith,

00:40:14.596 --> 00:40:17.791
who's gonna talk some about the Mobility
Division and

00:40:17.791 --> 00:40:21.063
the kinds of exciting work that's going on
there.

00:40:21.063 --> 00:40:29.225
[APPLAUSE].

00:40:29.225 --> 00:40:33.485
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon and thank you for coming
to the, the new longevity class, and

00:40:33.485 --> 00:40:36.279
thank you to Laura for that that lead-off.

00:40:36.279 --> 00:40:39.767
Always a great idea to put a speaker like
Laura up in front of a crowd and

00:40:39.767 --> 00:40:41.832
then say follow her with De engineer.

00:40:41.832 --> 00:40:45.633
So I, I hope that I can keep your interest
as well as she does here.

00:40:45.633 --> 00:40:51.570
So as she mentioned I direct the Mobility
Division at the Center on Longevity.

00:40:51.570 --> 00:40:55.630
And I'm gonna talk to you a little bit
today about what we do in that division.

00:40:55.630 --> 00:40:58.480
But then also I'm gonna dive a little
deeper and look at some of the data,

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:01.660
which I think is more fun if we're talking
about this being a class.

00:41:01.660 --> 00:41:04.700
So, let's get this out of the way at the
beginning in terms of what do

00:41:04.700 --> 00:41:05.640
we mean by mobility?

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:10.000
And I thought this was a really easy topic
when I started doing this job until I was

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:13.260
sitting down at one of the tech companies
in the Silicon Valley and after about 15

00:41:13.260 --> 00:41:16.316
minutes the guy looked at me and said,
this isn't about cell phones is it?

00:41:16.316 --> 00:41:22.690
[LAUGH]
So I think it's worth saying

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:26.960
that mobility in the way we look at it,
really is all about personal independence.

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:31.160
It's the ability to get around the world
on your own.

00:41:31.160 --> 00:41:33.090
To have self determination.

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:34.760
And the way we see to do that,

00:41:34.760 --> 00:41:39.630
is we look into ways in which we can keep
people mobile for as long as possible.

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:42.377
And we'll talk about prevention, and
exercise, and nutrition, and

00:41:42.377 --> 00:41:43.120
things like that.

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:48.840
We look at how to get people back to being
mobile when things go wrong.

00:41:48.840 --> 00:41:51.922
And honestly we do a little bit of work in
that area but

00:41:51.922 --> 00:41:56.760
the the medical school over across campus
does a great job in that area.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:42:00.760
And so, we find that we usually do our
best work where we are either

00:42:00.760 --> 00:42:04.440
looking across disciplines, or looking at
how we take Stanford research and

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:06.490
we help translate it to the outside.

00:42:06.490 --> 00:42:09.530
So there's a little bit there we can talk
about, but not as much.

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:13.370
And then finally, there are times when
mobility deficits happen.

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:16.246
People get hip replacements, people may be
in wheelchairs.

00:42:16.246 --> 00:42:22.922
And we looked for ways to continue to keep
people on a high quality of life, status.

00:42:22.922 --> 00:42:26.320
Even in the, in the face of those mobility
deficits, and

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:29.700
that often times means that we look at the
world around us, and

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:32.360
we look at ways that we can change the
world to accommodate the person.

00:42:33.460 --> 00:42:36.615
And, so, you can see as you look down
through here some of our,

00:42:36.615 --> 00:42:38.150
our research topics.

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:40.590
And I'm not actually gonna go through
those.

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:42.850
You know I could kinda go through each one
individually and

00:42:42.850 --> 00:42:46.560
give you kind of an inch deep view of each
thing that we're doing at the center.

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:49.430
But I thought it might be more interesting
to take a little bit of a deeper dive into

00:42:49.430 --> 00:42:51.790
one topic, show you a little bit of data,
and

00:42:51.790 --> 00:42:56.080
also maybe tell the story of how the
research that goes on at Stanford and

00:42:56.080 --> 00:42:59.790
elsewhere, together with the mission that
we've laid out, helps to

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:03.430
inform the choices that we make, the
things that we work on and where we go.

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:06.002
So we're gonna talk today-

00:43:06.002 --> 00:43:12.505
[CROSSTALK] About about activity and
fitness.

00:43:12.505 --> 00:43:17.018
And I'd suspect most of you have heard
talks about fitness and exercise, and

00:43:17.018 --> 00:43:20.770
I'm hoping that this is a little bit of an
exercise 2.0 talk,

00:43:20.770 --> 00:43:23.918
that's maybe different than the the
standard.

00:43:23.918 --> 00:43:25.809
So, I'm gonna start with an assumption
here.

00:43:25.809 --> 00:43:30.347
Is there anybody in the audience who would
like to raise their hand and

00:43:30.347 --> 00:43:33.100
convince me that exercise is bad for you?

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:33.959
Okay good.

00:43:33.959 --> 00:43:39.381
So we've eliminated that debate and we've
now gone over about 30 years of research.

00:43:39.381 --> 00:43:43.474
And we can, I think, all start from the
same grounding here,

00:43:43.474 --> 00:43:48.752
in that one of the more reliable findings
across across medical research is

00:43:48.752 --> 00:43:54.371
that exercise and fitness levels are good
for people in just a multitude of ways.

00:43:54.371 --> 00:43:57.701
And certainly, when you think about people
and aging, you start to

00:43:57.701 --> 00:44:01.861
think about chronic diseases, and there's
a whole list of chronic diseases.

00:44:01.861 --> 00:44:05.541
Cancer, diabetes, stroke, heart disease,

00:44:05.541 --> 00:44:11.220
that are all very positively correlated
with levels of fitness.

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:16.030
Increasingly over, maybe the last ten
years, it's become more evident that

00:44:16.030 --> 00:44:20.110
those levels of fitness are also
correlated with good cognitive results.

00:44:20.110 --> 00:44:26.150
And that exercise has in fact, in some
studies been shown to be as positive,

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:31.130
or to be as good as pharmaceuticals in
treating depression.

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:33.630
We see a high correlation of people who
are fit,

00:44:33.630 --> 00:44:37.384
with reduced instances of dementia.

00:44:37.384 --> 00:44:40.052
And even, even Alzheimer's, that Laura
mentioned.

00:44:40.052 --> 00:44:43.880
Doctor Frank Longo, who was over at the
medical school, and really one of

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:48.699
the top Alzheimer's researchers anywhere,
spoke at our design challenge last spring

00:44:48.699 --> 00:44:52.866
went through a whole host of data about
Alzheimer's and about possible cures.

00:44:52.866 --> 00:44:54.561
And at the end, he said.

00:44:54.561 --> 00:44:59.013
And by the way, there is, as of this
point, one treatment that is actually been

00:44:59.013 --> 00:45:03.410
scientifically shown to be correlated to
reduced instance of Alzheimer's.

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:06.390
It's a drug that's absolutely free.

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:08.842
It's available to everybody in society and

00:45:08.842 --> 00:45:12.608
we can't get anybody to take it, [LAUGH]
and, it's exercise.

00:45:12.608 --> 00:45:15.916
And really right now that is the, the one
the one finding for

00:45:15.916 --> 00:45:18.810
Alzheimer's that I think people tend to
agree on.

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:22.850
So, I'm gonna try to tell a little bit of
a story now,

00:45:22.850 --> 00:45:28.310
about where fitness research is going and
then where by extension some

00:45:28.310 --> 00:45:31.000
of the work that we're doing at the Center
on Longevity is gonna go.

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:35.660
And I’m gonna start with how we might find
a problem.

00:45:35.660 --> 00:45:39.600
So, Uri Ladabaum is a Gastroenterologist
and

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:43.670
a Professor of Medicine at Stanford, and
he published a study in the spring,

00:45:43.670 --> 00:45:48.560
and as Gastroenterologists are tending to
do, he looked at obesity.

00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:52.530
And, so he took a bunch of data from
something called the NHANES study, which

00:45:52.530 --> 00:45:57.890
is a National Health and Nutrition Survey,
and looked at self reports from people

00:45:57.890 --> 00:46:03.540
about how much exercise they got, as well
as how many calories they were taking in.

00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:07.040
And being a guy that cared about belly fat
and things that like, he was looking to

00:46:07.040 --> 00:46:11.530
see if he could find reasons for why
people were getting more and more obese.

00:46:11.530 --> 00:46:15.100
And you may in fact have seen some of this
work that came out last spring,

00:46:15.100 --> 00:46:16.510
I guess March.

00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:19.940
And the press picked it up and plastered
all these headlines out there that says,

00:46:19.940 --> 00:46:23.400
exercise is more important than diet and
preventing obesity.

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:27.070
Which if you go back to the source, Uri
tells you is patently incorrect.

00:46:28.410 --> 00:46:29.790
So the press got it wrong on this one.

00:46:29.790 --> 00:46:34.960
And if fact it's a very very complicated
in combination between the two.

00:46:34.960 --> 00:46:37.740
And I don't think anybody right now claims
that they understand the answer

00:46:37.740 --> 00:46:38.920
to that question.

00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:43.010
But, what came out of the study that
really leaped off the page to us,

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:47.930
was this this reported data about how much
physical activity people were

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:49.490
getting in their leisure time.

00:46:49.490 --> 00:46:53.000
So what I'm showing here are for women
over 18, women over 65,

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:54.440
but really, you can look across men,

00:46:54.440 --> 00:46:57.650
you can look across different ethnicities,
doesn't really matter what the cohort is.

00:46:57.650 --> 00:46:59.620
This data looks pretty much the same.

00:46:59.620 --> 00:47:03.200
And they broke it down into saying, did
you get the ideal amount of exercise?

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:06.270
So, the kind of thing that would be
recommended by like the American College

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:07.560
of Sports Medicine.

00:47:07.560 --> 00:47:09.060
Did you get intermediate exercise?

00:47:09.060 --> 00:47:13.290
So basically some exercise, or did you get
nothing?

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:15.570
And what you see here in the blue data,

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:19.712
is what was recorded back in a window
between 1988 and 1994.

00:47:19.712 --> 00:47:25.070
What you see in the orange data is what's
being recorded in 2009 and 2010.

00:47:25.070 --> 00:47:30.060
So the upshot here is that over twice as
many people, in this particular cohort,

00:47:30.060 --> 00:47:32.320
but it holds pretty much across,

00:47:32.320 --> 00:47:36.240
are responding that they're getting no
exercise whatsoever.

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:37.300
So that's, you know,

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:41.680
two to three times, right, of the number
of people across the population.

00:47:41.680 --> 00:47:44.770
And, you know, that's a huge number, so
that jumps out at us and

00:47:44.770 --> 00:47:47.420
we say well, you know, we should maybe pay
some attention to that.

00:47:47.420 --> 00:47:49.440
Now, why should we pay attention to that?

00:47:51.080 --> 00:47:53.090
So there's, there's a lot of papers, as I
mentioned,

00:47:53.090 --> 00:47:56.290
about linking chronic disease with with
fitness.

00:47:56.290 --> 00:47:59.330
But, I pulled these two charts off of the
website for

00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:02.330
the Center of Disease Control, actually
off of different places.

00:48:02.330 --> 00:48:05.040
And I thought a picture was worth a
thousand words in this case.

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:08.190
What you see on the right here are people
reporting,

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:11.490
sort of the percentage of the population
that is reporting no physical activity,

00:48:11.490 --> 00:48:16.450
in the darker colors are higher rates of
inactivity, so less exercise.

00:48:16.450 --> 00:48:21.599
On the right, it's the quartiles of the
country broken out in terms of rates of

00:48:21.599 --> 00:48:26.139
chronic disease, and when you overlay
those two, it's striking.

00:48:27.626 --> 00:48:30.520
So, I think if you live in the US,
particularly if you live in

00:48:30.520 --> 00:48:35.270
the south apparently, you should care
pretty deeply about physical inactivity.

00:48:35.270 --> 00:48:38.780
And you know, the amount of, the quality
of life degradations that would go

00:48:38.780 --> 00:48:41.910
along with this are huge for our
population as a whole.

00:48:41.910 --> 00:48:43.610
So now we as a center look at that and say
well,

00:48:43.610 --> 00:48:46.550
you know, if the problem isn't that we
gotta go get people to run marathons.

00:48:46.550 --> 00:48:51.190
That we need to get people to get up from
in front of the TV and do something.

00:48:51.190 --> 00:48:52.780
Okay, now we're talking about a problem a,

00:48:52.780 --> 00:48:56.140
a problem that maybe we can take a look at
and start to, to make some impact.

00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:01.820
So, that kind of leads to the next
question, is if we don't need people to

00:49:01.820 --> 00:49:05.646
run marathons, how much do we need people
to exercise?

00:49:05.646 --> 00:49:07.753
And this data was not, did not come from
Stanford,

00:49:07.753 --> 00:49:11.340
came from the place called the Pennington
Biomedical Research Center in Baton Rouge.

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:16.140
May not be a household word but these
folks have been doing some of the better

00:49:16.140 --> 00:49:20.890
exercise physiology work since I think
they were founded in 1980.

00:49:20.890 --> 00:49:24.880
And what this shows without trying to go
into all of the lines here,

00:49:24.880 --> 00:49:30.380
is both the top and the bottom graphs are
talking about rates of mortality.

00:49:30.380 --> 00:49:33.218
So once again we're talking about what's
your chance of dying.

00:49:33.218 --> 00:49:35.748
And on the top, it's basically due to
anything.

00:49:35.748 --> 00:49:39.277
On the bottom graph, it's due just to to
heart disease.

00:49:39.277 --> 00:49:41.159
Cardiovascular problems.

00:49:41.159 --> 00:49:45.714
And what you're seeing as you go from left
to right on this graph, is the group, and

00:49:45.714 --> 00:49:50.350
this was over 55,000 individuals, so it's,
it's not a small study.

00:49:50.350 --> 00:49:54.620
Looking at by quintiles how much exercise
people are getting.

00:49:54.620 --> 00:49:56.070
And the various lines mean different
things.

00:49:56.070 --> 00:49:57.340
And these are runners by the way.

00:49:57.340 --> 00:50:01.010
So we're talking specifically about how
much people are running.

00:50:01.010 --> 00:50:06.000
And, you know, one line is about how long
you run, how far, how fast, how often.

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:09.410
But as it turns out, it doesn't matter all
that much in terms of,

00:50:09.410 --> 00:50:12.210
as long as you're getting a certain amount
of running in in the week,

00:50:12.210 --> 00:50:16.020
the drop in mortality rate happens and it
happens really quickly.

00:50:16.020 --> 00:50:20.460
The biggest drops that you see up there
are between doing nothing and

00:50:20.460 --> 00:50:22.650
the bottom 20% of the population.

00:50:23.754 --> 00:50:26.994
Now in fact this study is not without
controversy as people at

00:50:26.994 --> 00:50:29.800
Stanford have let me know if I talk about
this study.

00:50:31.490 --> 00:50:34.240
We have some great exercise physiology
researchers at Stanford,

00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:37.110
and they've pointed out that the right
hand of that graph

00:50:37.110 --> 00:50:39.500
doesn't necessarily correlate with what we
know.

00:50:39.500 --> 00:50:40.730
We know that if you do, say,

00:50:40.730 --> 00:50:43.700
more running, that your cardiovascular
health gets better.

00:50:43.700 --> 00:50:47.010
When your cardiovascular health gets
better, you got less of a chance of dying.

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:50.040
So, I would say, if you took the middle of
that graph and look to the right.

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:51.280
That's controversial.

00:50:51.280 --> 00:50:54.560
If you take the middle of the graph and
you look to the left if not, and

00:50:54.560 --> 00:50:56.560
everybody would agree with that piece of
it.

00:50:56.560 --> 00:51:00.460
So now we've said we have a big problem,
the problem is people don't do things.

00:51:00.460 --> 00:51:02.420
And you really don't have to do all that
much.

00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:07.170
So this is that bottom quintile is
equivalent roughly 10 minutes

00:51:07.170 --> 00:51:07.719
a day of running.

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:10.920
And it really doesn't matter kinda how
many days a week you run.

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:14.050
It's just, sort of, are you getting your
15 minutes of running in per week?

00:51:14.050 --> 00:51:15.130
That's a big number.

00:51:15.130 --> 00:51:17.318
I mean, I'm sorry, a small number having a
big impact.

00:51:17.318 --> 00:51:21.033
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:51:21.033 --> 00:51:24.255
So when I show this, most people
immediately respond, oh God,

00:51:24.255 --> 00:51:25.096
do I have to run?

00:51:25.096 --> 00:51:25.630
I hate to run.

00:51:25.630 --> 00:51:27.464
Please don't make me run.

00:51:27.464 --> 00:51:33.409
and, and people and really, I think,
everybody would agree.

00:51:33.409 --> 00:51:37.711
You could replace running with swimming or
biking or any, any vigorous exercise.

00:51:37.711 --> 00:51:40.339
But most people wanna know about walking.

00:51:40.339 --> 00:51:43.674
And this data is actually some work that
was done in Taiwan to

00:51:43.674 --> 00:51:46.750
look at the equivalency between running
and walking.

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:47.880
So if you're going to go out and do a
walk.

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:51.070
And you're worried about that ten minutes
of running a day you maybe don't

00:51:51.070 --> 00:51:55.310
wanna run ten min, walk ten minutes, you
probably wanna walk 25 or 30 minutes.

00:51:55.310 --> 00:51:57.110
The good side of that is that, and

00:51:57.110 --> 00:52:00.110
again, we're looking at mortality
reduction again.

00:52:00.110 --> 00:52:01.710
The good news is, you can get there by
walking.

00:52:01.710 --> 00:52:03.340
You may have to spend a little bit more
time on it.

00:52:08.230 --> 00:52:11.393
So that's where we stand in terms of
exercise and the research there.

00:52:11.393 --> 00:52:15.170
Now I wanna layer on top of that, an
opportunity that we're seeing, and

00:52:15.170 --> 00:52:21.200
in this case, this is an opportunity that
isn't so much being led my academia,

00:52:21.200 --> 00:52:24.010
although their is certainly a lot of good
work going on there.

00:52:24.010 --> 00:52:26.930
But I would say that this is one where
industry is probably ahead of academia,

00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:31.060
and that's in this area of, of data
acquisition.

00:52:31.060 --> 00:52:33.270
And how do you acquire data about yourself
and

00:52:33.270 --> 00:52:36.330
particularly about the activities that you
do during a day?

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:38.290
So some of you may have seen these.

00:52:38.290 --> 00:52:39.840
That's the Fitbit on the left or

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:42.690
the Nike FuelBand in the middle or a
pedometer on the right.

00:52:43.710 --> 00:52:47.160
And a lot of people now are able to get
individualized data on themselves and

00:52:47.160 --> 00:52:50.240
start to personalize how they think about
their own activity.

00:52:50.240 --> 00:52:55.100
So, last year I had the opportunity to go
to the Consumer Electronics Show in

00:52:55.100 --> 00:52:58.720
Las Vegas and part of my goal there was to
see what was being done in the space.

00:52:58.720 --> 00:53:03.110
And you know those are the kinda things
that I had heard about.

00:53:03.110 --> 00:53:05.490
Well it turned out that there was about
three or

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:09.260
four aisles of company's doing this in one
way or another.

00:53:09.260 --> 00:53:12.070
Now a lot of this data is kind of the same
data over and over again.

00:53:12.070 --> 00:53:13.610
A lot of it's accelerometer data,

00:53:13.610 --> 00:53:17.400
basically how much movement do you get in
general and cutoff rates.

00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:20.200
But there's another level of these coming
down the road.

00:53:20.200 --> 00:53:23.530
In fact, some of them have just launched
in the last few weeks that measure things

00:53:23.530 --> 00:53:29.760
like heart rate skin moisture level, how
much are you sweating sleep quality.

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:32.710
And so, we had this really increasing
ability to

00:53:32.710 --> 00:53:34.150
acquire personal data on people.

00:53:34.150 --> 00:53:37.380
In fact, there's a post doc researcher
here at Standford,

00:53:37.380 --> 00:53:40.380
Mary Rosenbarger, who's over in the
Psychology Department, that's been

00:53:40.380 --> 00:53:44.960
trying to kinda keep a handle on this, and
track which devices are good for what.

00:53:44.960 --> 00:53:47.940
And I had a chat with her a few weeks ago
and she said I'm drowning here.

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:50.950
Since spring, there's been 40 new devices
launched in this category.

00:53:52.180 --> 00:53:57.180
So, if you start to think about, how do
you get people to exercise,

00:53:57.180 --> 00:54:01.350
how much do you have to exercise, now
you've got some tools to start reporting.

00:54:01.350 --> 00:54:02.760
We aren't at the end of the story here.

00:54:02.760 --> 00:54:05.490
We're kind of at the beginning, but we
think we have the basis to

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:09.040
actually start looking at, how do you
actually get people to do stuff?

00:54:09.040 --> 00:54:12.350
And I think that's where the really
interesting work is gonna come

00:54:12.350 --> 00:54:13.520
over the next year or two.

00:54:13.520 --> 00:54:17.210
And we're doing a couple of things at the
Center on Longevity that's go,

00:54:17.210 --> 00:54:18.740
that are going that direction.

00:54:18.740 --> 00:54:21.680
So, the President’s Council on Fitness,
Sports and

00:54:21.680 --> 00:54:24.500
Nutrition, I think probably most of you
have heard about that.

00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:27.590
This is the group that they kid about it
themselves, about the group that's

00:54:27.590 --> 00:54:30.295
having you to pushups in gym class since
the Eisenhower administration.

00:54:30.295 --> 00:54:31.930
[LAUGH].

00:54:31.930 --> 00:54:36.870
And, they, their, their their corner stone
project has always been

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:39.900
the President Physical fitness challenge,
and they have focused pretty much

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:45.130
solely since their founding on children in
K through 12.

00:54:45.130 --> 00:54:48.800
Well, recently they've paid attention to
the same data that Laura was talking

00:54:48.800 --> 00:54:52.550
about, and said you know, we need to start
expanding our charter because one thing I

00:54:52.550 --> 00:54:56.040
actually didn't say about the exercise
data was that, not only is it good for

00:54:56.040 --> 00:54:57.800
you, it's good for you at all ages.

00:54:57.800 --> 00:55:00.700
And really the data says, it doesn't so
much matter when you start.

00:55:00.700 --> 00:55:03.550
I mean, obviously it's better to start
earlier, cuz things don't develop.

00:55:03.550 --> 00:55:07.480
But even if you start later, you're still
gonna see significant benefits.

00:55:07.480 --> 00:55:08.860
And so, they have said,

00:55:08.860 --> 00:55:12.650
we need to figure out what we need to tell
older people about exercise.

00:55:12.650 --> 00:55:15.740
And, so we talked with them and it was the
federal government.

00:55:15.740 --> 00:55:22.340
So in a short nine months later we managed
to sign a formal memorandum

00:55:22.340 --> 00:55:26.400
of understanding between Stanford and the
government to work with them to help

00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:30.710
define what that messaging, what those
programs are gonna be looking forward.

00:55:30.710 --> 00:55:32.280
So, over the next year or

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:36.320
so, we're gonna be gathering together some
of the best scientists.

00:55:36.320 --> 00:55:39.890
And this is not all about just exercise
physiology medicine, and

00:55:39.890 --> 00:55:43.520
this is why Stanford is so wonderful, and
it's a great place to do this work,

00:55:43.520 --> 00:55:45.710
is because you go wandering into any
department, and

00:55:45.710 --> 00:55:49.430
you pretty much find somebody who is one
of the best in the world at what they do.

00:55:49.430 --> 00:55:53.090
So we've got people signed up to work on
this who are in computer science,

00:55:53.090 --> 00:55:57.480
who are in communications, who are in
medicine, who are in.

00:55:57.480 --> 00:56:00.520
You know, the Director of Sports Medicine
at Stanford is interested.

00:56:00.520 --> 00:56:03.200
So, there is a help group at Stanford
that's going to

00:56:03.200 --> 00:56:06.120
come together over the next year or so,
and try to put something together and

00:56:06.120 --> 00:56:08.930
hopefully you'll see some things coming
out of that, you know, in the nearer term.

00:56:08.930 --> 00:56:11.140
Because it is the federal government, and

00:56:11.140 --> 00:56:13.350
the contract runs out when the
administration switches, so.

00:56:15.580 --> 00:56:16.100
The second thing I

00:56:16.100 --> 00:56:18.910
wanted to talk about was the Center on
Longevity design challenge.

00:56:18.910 --> 00:56:24.300
This is actually year two of our
challenge, and what we've done is,

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:27.482
it's a two prong thing here, two reasons
we wanna do this.

00:56:27.482 --> 00:56:29.470
One, we would like to get some great
ideas, and

00:56:29.470 --> 00:56:33.400
we think young people in universities have
great ideas.

00:56:33.400 --> 00:56:37.835
But secondarily, we are an academic
institution and we believe that aging,

00:56:37.835 --> 00:56:41.210
and aging of all populations is one of the
great tends of our century,

00:56:41.210 --> 00:56:44.660
really of our lifetime, as Laura has
talked about.

00:56:44.660 --> 00:56:49.190
But you go talk to a designer, or
engineering student, or a, a psychologist

00:56:49.190 --> 00:56:53.300
who is 20 years old, and it's not the
first thing that people will go to.

00:56:53.300 --> 00:56:54.130
Right?

00:56:54.130 --> 00:56:56.210
I really wanna work on things for old
people.

00:56:58.450 --> 00:57:01.030
So to motivate them to start thinking
about that,

00:57:01.030 --> 00:57:03.410
and really to realize what the
opportunities are in this space.

00:57:03.410 --> 00:57:05.490
Because the opportunities are big.

00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:10.700
We have sent this challenge out to any,
any accredited university in the world,

00:57:10.700 --> 00:57:15.120
to come up with solutions to reduce
sedentary lifestyles,

00:57:15.120 --> 00:57:17.990
encourage physical movement and exercise
and reduce barriers.

00:57:19.010 --> 00:57:22.650
We give out some cash prizes, but really
the hook here,

00:57:22.650 --> 00:57:25.910
especially as you start to go farther and
farther away from California,

00:57:25.910 --> 00:57:30.140
the hook here is that we, we take all of
the inputs we get in December.

00:57:30.140 --> 00:57:32.540
We cut it down to a group of finalists
between like five and

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:33.660
eight finalist teams.

00:57:33.660 --> 00:57:36.158
And we fly those teams to Stanford in
April to present.

00:57:36.158 --> 00:57:39.590
So we pair 'em up with industry so they
get some mentorship.

00:57:39.590 --> 00:57:43.040
But then we bring 'em to Stanford and we
put 'em in front of a group of investors,

00:57:43.040 --> 00:57:44.870
in front of a group of companies.

00:57:44.870 --> 00:57:49.380
And if you don't think that somebody that
is in India thinks it would be pretty cool

00:57:49.380 --> 00:57:54.520
to get a ticket to Stanford and present to
Silicon Valley, it's a real motivator.

00:57:54.520 --> 00:57:57.215
So as you see, we are going to be doing
this across the,

00:57:57.215 --> 00:58:01.550
the breadth of the year and this is our,
as I mentioned, this is our

00:58:01.550 --> 00:58:06.066
second year and we really do think that
this is, we've caught on to a trend here.

00:58:06.066 --> 00:58:09.660
We did this last year and our topic,

00:58:09.660 --> 00:58:12.270
instead of physical issues was cognitive
issues.

00:58:12.270 --> 00:58:13.810
So we had a challenge for

00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:18.430
solutions that would help people remain
independent with cognitive impairment.

00:58:18.430 --> 00:58:21.340
And this is a representation of the 52
teams that entered and

00:58:21.340 --> 00:58:25.610
where they came from across the world.

00:58:25.610 --> 00:58:30.420
And really we didn't have much budget so
we did this via emails and Facebook and

00:58:30.420 --> 00:58:33.650
Twitter and kind of a grass roots
movement.

00:58:33.650 --> 00:58:38.130
And this year because we know till
December how many we get

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:40.280
we believe it's going to be a bigger
group.

00:58:40.280 --> 00:58:45.180
The only metric I have is that about this
year last, this time last year we had

00:58:45.180 --> 00:58:49.710
about a 140, 150 people registered for
updates on our challenge.

00:58:49.710 --> 00:58:50.840
It's at 550 this year.

00:58:50.840 --> 00:58:54.124
So we'll have to see what we get.

00:58:54.124 --> 00:58:58.530
I'm just gonna close here by showing you
our winner from last here to get us

00:58:58.530 --> 00:59:02.439
a sense of not only the breadth of, of
where things came from, but

00:59:02.439 --> 00:59:06.666
the quality level of what we got which
really was kind of amazing.

00:59:06.666 --> 00:59:08.357
This is a young woman from,

00:59:08.357 --> 00:59:13.081
of all places, the Academy of Art
Institute, Institute in San Francisco.

00:59:13.081 --> 00:59:15.506
So here we were thinking we were gonna get
all engineers and

00:59:15.506 --> 00:59:17.409
we ended up with an artist winning it.

00:59:17.409 --> 00:59:21.988
But what she did was the most beautiful
redesign of a set of dishware for

00:59:21.988 --> 00:59:23.856
people with Alzheimer's.

00:59:23.856 --> 00:59:26.288
And her grandmother has Alzheimer's, and

00:59:26.288 --> 00:59:29.440
she spent a lot of time working with
people.

00:59:29.440 --> 00:59:32.050
And so I'll point out a couple of things
that she found.

00:59:32.050 --> 00:59:33.270
You'll see, people will look at that and

00:59:33.270 --> 00:59:37.298
say boy that's kinda a silly color scheme,
you know, it really looks primary.

00:59:37.298 --> 00:59:39.803
She went and visited Alzheimer's
facilities and

00:59:39.803 --> 00:59:43.973
found that a lot of times these facilities
will have these very nice plates that have

00:59:43.973 --> 00:59:47.448
flowers on the bottom and things like
that, because they look nice.

00:59:47.448 --> 00:59:50.385
People with Alzheimer's will confuse those
flowers on the bottom of

00:59:50.385 --> 00:59:53.740
the plate with food, and she'd find people
trying to eat out of an empty bowl.

00:59:53.740 --> 00:59:57.605
So, she redesigned her dishes so that the
bowl is, in the bottom of the bowl is

00:59:57.605 --> 01:00:00.950
a color that, essentially, no food you
would wanna eat comes in.

01:00:03.020 --> 01:00:05.670
The bottom, she found people spilling soup
trying to get that last bit out.

01:00:05.670 --> 01:00:07.800
So, she just put an angle in the bottom of
the bowl.

01:00:07.800 --> 01:00:09.500
She found that people hold their spoons
like this.

01:00:09.500 --> 01:00:10.220
So, she changed that.

01:00:10.220 --> 01:00:14.130
So, she went through each piece and did,
did this redesign.

01:00:14.130 --> 01:00:17.500
And, so we've been working oh, with her
over the last year to try to

01:00:17.500 --> 01:00:20.860
figure out how do we actually bring this
thing forward.

01:00:20.860 --> 01:00:23.990
And actually, just roughly 36 hours ago
she,

01:00:23.990 --> 01:00:28.060
she launched a campaign on the
crowdfunding site Indiegogo which is

01:00:28.060 --> 01:00:31.610
a lot like Kickstarter, if you're familiar
with that one.

01:00:31.610 --> 01:00:32.930
And in the first 36 hours,

01:00:32.930 --> 01:00:36.210
she's now just over a quarter of the way
to her $70,000 goal.

01:00:37.250 --> 01:00:39.140
So, we're very encouraged by that.

01:00:39.140 --> 01:00:42.570
We'd like to keep following this and the
70,000, by the way,

01:00:42.570 --> 01:00:45.750
is just literally to pay for the tooling
so she can injection mold the parts and

01:00:45.750 --> 01:00:46.679
start selling the parts.

01:00:48.020 --> 01:00:49.653
So, really, this is what we hope to see,

01:00:49.653 --> 01:00:52.178
which is to have, let young people bring
these ideas forward and

01:00:52.178 --> 01:00:55.002
find a way to keep them, you know, keep
them going forward from there.

01:00:55.002 --> 01:01:02.764
So, I will close with just one slide that
summarizes what we're up against here.

01:01:02.764 --> 01:01:08.311
And [LAUGH] and with that, I'm, I'm gonna
pass this off to my colleague,

01:01:08.311 --> 01:01:12.037
Martha Deevy, and take some questions, I
think,

01:01:12.037 --> 01:01:15.640
when we're up in the, we're up at the end
here.

01:01:15.640 --> 01:01:17.380
So, Martha, I'd like to turn this over to
you.

01:01:17.380 --> 01:01:18.060
Thank you.

01:01:18.060 --> 01:01:19.060
&gt;&gt; Wait, don't go yet.

01:01:19.060 --> 01:01:21.605
Where's mine?
[APPLAUSE].

01:01:21.605 --> 01:01:26.520
[APPLAUSE]
You know, Ken, Laura, and a number of

01:01:26.520 --> 01:01:32.370
other [UNKNOWN] longevity colleagues do
these speak, speeches fairly frequently.

01:01:32.370 --> 01:01:36.160
And we're always figuring out who can not
follow Laura.

01:01:37.730 --> 01:01:38.580
Who can go first?

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:42.020
Laura presents a really wonderful,
optimistic picture of the future.

01:01:42.020 --> 01:01:44.760
That optimistic future, the optimistic
picture of

01:01:44.760 --> 01:01:47.990
the future is the reason why I left
industry and came to the center.

01:01:47.990 --> 01:01:52.120
But, sitting here for the last couple of
minutes I realized Laura starts off with

01:01:52.120 --> 01:01:56.710
just how wonderful these new, these
additional years will be.

01:01:56.710 --> 01:02:03.560
Ken then gets to talk about cool
technology and shoes, and all new gadgets.

01:02:03.560 --> 01:02:04.130
And, oh, by the way,

01:02:04.130 --> 01:02:06.141
you don't have to exercise as much as
we've been telling you.

01:02:06.141 --> 01:02:11.123
So, I run the financial security program
at the center.

01:02:11.123 --> 01:02:15.610
[LAUGH]
And I could probably make my comments,

01:02:15.610 --> 01:02:18.870
complete my comments in about, in about
ten seconds.

01:02:18.870 --> 01:02:21.390
We have to work longer, and we have to
save more.

01:02:21.390 --> 01:02:22.035
Thank you very much.

01:02:22.035 --> 01:02:27.273
[LAUGH] [APPLAUSE] I will take a few
minutes,

01:02:27.273 --> 01:02:32.820
though, to go through some of the details.

01:02:34.980 --> 01:02:38.040
In a very optimistic way, I just actually
looked at a couple of headlines in,

01:02:38.040 --> 01:02:41.390
that were in the ne, in the news for the
la, over the last week or so.

01:02:41.390 --> 01:02:46.070
And all we hear about is the problems with
this looming

01:02:46.070 --> 01:02:50.560
retirement crisis caused by the baby
boomers were not leaving the work force.

01:02:50.560 --> 01:02:53.480
We haven't saved enough, we're gonna
bankrupt social security.

01:02:53.480 --> 01:02:54.850
And that's what you hear about.

01:02:55.970 --> 01:02:57.170
And there's some truth to it.

01:02:57.170 --> 01:02:58.680
I mean, we are living longer.

01:02:59.940 --> 01:03:05.060
And by the way, we're living longer but
we're retiring at about the same

01:03:05.060 --> 01:03:11.000
rates we used to, and this projection out
to 2050 indicates that, ultimately,

01:03:11.000 --> 01:03:16.040
we're gonna be living a full 22 years in
retirement, whereas as recently as 9,

01:03:16.040 --> 01:03:18.810
1950, we were living 9 years in
retirement.

01:03:18.810 --> 01:03:22.670
John Chovin, our, our economist colleague
over at Seprin, our,

01:03:22.670 --> 01:03:26.540
my faculty adviser, my program's faculty
adviser is known for saying you

01:03:26.540 --> 01:03:30.800
cannot finance a 30 year career, a 30 year
retirement with a 40 year career.

01:03:33.685 --> 01:03:35.251
Yet, that's exactly what we're trying to
do.

01:03:35.251 --> 01:03:39.246
[BLANK_AUDIO]

01:03:39.246 --> 01:03:42.258
So, I spend a lot of time trying to
understand well,

01:03:42.258 --> 01:03:47.190
how do we help people think differently
about preparing for retirement?

01:03:47.190 --> 01:03:51.150
They understand, at some level, that
they're more responsible today than they

01:03:51.150 --> 01:03:56.410
ever have been to prepare for their own
retirement, but yet, you know,

01:03:56.410 --> 01:03:59.970
as if they're facing many other difficult
decisions, they don't take action.

01:03:59.970 --> 01:04:02.738
These numbers I've often found pretty
staggering.

01:04:02.738 --> 01:04:05.460
Only 1 third of adults in their 50s have
ever tried

01:04:06.720 --> 01:04:09.015
to calculate what they need in retirement.

01:04:09.015 --> 01:04:17.648
More than half haven't even attempted to
put pen to paper.

01:04:17.648 --> 01:04:20.180
44% of the boomers by, there are three or

01:04:20.180 --> 01:04:24.510
four fairly well publicized surveys about
retirement confidence.

01:04:24.510 --> 01:04:31.920
And you'll see percentages ranging from
44% to 55% people surveyed 55 years and

01:04:31.920 --> 01:04:37.940
over all levels of affluence indicating
that they are not confident or

01:04:37.940 --> 01:04:43.765
not confident at all, that they will be
able to live comfortably in retirement.

01:04:43.765 --> 01:04:47.190
43% have less than $25,000 in retirement
savings.

01:04:49.440 --> 01:04:51.540
Now, by virtue of the fact that you are
sitting in this room,

01:04:51.540 --> 01:04:54.190
I am assuming that you are not part of
that population.

01:04:57.250 --> 01:05:01.911
And 14% of middle income boomers don't
have any retirement income at all.

01:05:01.911 --> 01:05:06.115
[BLANK_AUDIO]

01:05:06.115 --> 01:05:11.032
So, in addition to that, every other
element of the plan, so

01:05:11.032 --> 01:05:15.220
I am a boomer, I got my first job in 1983.

01:05:15.220 --> 01:05:16.720
My first job had a pension.

01:05:18.300 --> 01:05:20.850
The only thing I was asked to do was put
it, sign on the bottom line.

01:05:20.850 --> 01:05:25.150
I wasn't asked to allocate anything and I
was told in my new employ,

01:05:25.150 --> 01:05:31.300
employment orientation that if I stayed
with Zenith Electronics until I was 65,

01:05:31.300 --> 01:05:33.419
everything would take care of itself.

01:05:36.340 --> 01:05:39.673
Well, I was probably the last generation
to ever be,

01:05:39.673 --> 01:05:44.080
last person in my generation to ever be
presented with that opportunity.

01:05:44.080 --> 01:05:45.550
By the way, they took it away five years
later.

01:05:47.330 --> 01:05:52.260
But we're now thinking that the three legs
to the stool that we assume we have to

01:05:52.260 --> 01:05:55.630
have in place to get ready for retirement,
two of the pillars, two of the,

01:05:55.630 --> 01:05:57.929
two of the legs are kind of out of
balance.

01:05:59.110 --> 01:06:02.760
Is Social Security gonna be around when
you reach retirement age?

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:05.380
Now, this particular, these particular
statistics obviously are for

01:06:05.380 --> 01:06:06.490
younger population.

01:06:08.010 --> 01:06:13.010
Those in my age group believe that it will
be around in some way, shape, or form.

01:06:13.010 --> 01:06:15.500
Though a large number of us are doubting
whether or

01:06:15.500 --> 01:06:17.790
not it'll be there the way we expect it
will be.

01:06:20.330 --> 01:06:21.910
The shift in defined benefit,

01:06:21.910 --> 01:06:25.280
defined contribution plans is really
pretty fou, profound.

01:06:25.280 --> 01:06:27.680
It began in earnest in the mid 80s.

01:06:27.680 --> 01:06:28.350
But right now,

01:06:28.350 --> 01:06:33.400
2012, there are only 10% only 10% of
companies out there offer a pension plan.

01:06:34.600 --> 01:06:38.084
So, we're now relying more and more on
personal savings.

01:06:38.084 --> 01:06:42.066
[BLANK_AUDIO]

01:06:42.066 --> 01:06:44.764
So, we at the center tra, took a step back
and

01:06:44.764 --> 01:06:48.172
said, well, how do you help people think
differently?

01:06:48.172 --> 01:06:52.932
And I really do have a lifespan view of
this process.

01:06:52.932 --> 01:06:56.490
This is a process that needs to start as
soon as you get your first job.

01:06:58.230 --> 01:06:59.970
And we think it's a process where,

01:06:59.970 --> 01:07:05.710
one, people need to be, learn to be good
consumers of financial resources.

01:07:05.710 --> 01:07:08.922
Note that I'm not saying learn how to be a
great portfolio manager.

01:07:08.922 --> 01:07:11.023
[BLANK_AUDIO]

01:07:11.023 --> 01:07:13.810
Second, you need to recognize that you
need to earn income.

01:07:13.810 --> 01:07:15.279
And you need to earn income for a while.

01:07:17.960 --> 01:07:21.770
Third, you need to save for the future,
but you need to save for emergencies.

01:07:21.770 --> 01:07:24.720
Historically, we've talked about
retirement planning as simply the event

01:07:24.720 --> 01:07:25.650
called retirement.

01:07:27.350 --> 01:07:29.790
Yet, so many things happen before that.

01:07:29.790 --> 01:07:31.710
And often, the impediments to saving for

01:07:31.710 --> 01:07:34.200
retirement are those intermediate
decisions.

01:07:34.200 --> 01:07:35.190
I wanna buy a house.

01:07:35.190 --> 01:07:36.790
I wanna send my kids to school.

01:07:36.790 --> 01:07:38.060
I'm carrying a lot of student debt.

01:07:39.530 --> 01:07:43.640
Those all need to be factored in this
concept of preparing for retirement.

01:07:43.640 --> 01:07:47.270
Cuz until you can navigate through some of
those other steps,

01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:48.570
you're not gonna think about retirement.

01:07:50.460 --> 01:07:51.800
You have to think about investing in

01:07:51.800 --> 01:07:54.020
assets that actually will generate
something.

01:07:55.120 --> 01:08:00.420
Will generate income at the end and
finally, you need to protect those assets.

01:08:00.420 --> 01:08:04.956
So, we have, we've defined three areas of
focus at the center, one is financial

01:08:04.956 --> 01:08:09.453
capability, the second is working longer,
and the third is fraud prevention.

01:08:09.453 --> 01:08:13.452
Financial capability is really
understanding how to be a good consumer of

01:08:13.452 --> 01:08:14.749
financial resources.

01:08:15.940 --> 01:08:18.260
Working longer is pretty much what it
says.

01:08:18.260 --> 01:08:20.580
We need to think about working longer.

01:08:20.580 --> 01:08:21.475
And the third is,

01:08:21.475 --> 01:08:24.830
looking at how to protect the assets that
you, that you acquired.

01:08:27.760 --> 01:08:32.200
So, the shift from defined benefit plans
to defined contribution plans,

01:08:32.200 --> 01:08:36.750
in the mid 80s, led to a proliferation of
financial literacy programs.

01:08:37.954 --> 01:08:41.804
And I thought it'd be interesting to just
spend a minute,

01:08:41.804 --> 01:08:46.753
kind of 20 years into this experiment, to
really just touch on a couple of

01:08:46.753 --> 01:08:50.919
studies that are beginning to question
whether we're doing,

01:08:50.919 --> 01:08:54.717
whether we're approaching this problem the
right way.

01:08:54.717 --> 01:08:59.011
That first study, financial literacy,
financial education, economic outcomes,

01:08:59.011 --> 01:09:03.006
was a survey study that looked at programs
out in marketplace, and really said,

01:09:03.006 --> 01:09:06.534
are they accomplishing the goal of
improving the financial outcomes of

01:09:06.534 --> 01:09:07.557
the participants.

01:09:07.557 --> 01:09:11.160
And at the end of it, they said, it's
inconclusive.

01:09:11.160 --> 01:09:16.930
Second study, financial education,
financial competence, and consumer welfare

01:09:16.930 --> 01:09:20.160
actually looked at programs and said, are
the me,

01:09:20.160 --> 01:09:25.160
we are teaching people certain, certain
elements of financial literacy.

01:09:25.160 --> 01:09:26.350
How well are they learning those?

01:09:27.390 --> 01:09:28.020
And oh, by the,

01:09:28.020 --> 01:09:33.660
by the way, are those, is that
understanding indicative of good outcomes?

01:09:33.660 --> 01:09:36.600
Discover that people are learning.

01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:41.720
That when people learn, and respond, and
can understand the elements of

01:09:41.720 --> 01:09:45.850
financial literacy, it actually does not
improve the outcomes.

01:09:47.590 --> 01:09:48.820
So we are teaching the wrong things.

01:09:51.370 --> 01:09:56.700
The third, which is interesting in, in
this sort of environment of social media,

01:09:56.700 --> 01:10:01.450
and sharing everything with everybody, it
was a study where information was

01:10:01.450 --> 01:10:07.040
shared with lower income earning
employees, about what their peers, how,

01:10:07.040 --> 01:10:08.650
how their peers were saving for
retirement.

01:10:10.220 --> 01:10:14.260
And not only did that not motivate
employees to

01:10:14.260 --> 01:10:18.630
save more, it demotivated them.

01:10:18.630 --> 01:10:24.850
So, that's leading, you know, those of us
who spend a lot of time in this

01:10:24.850 --> 01:10:29.220
space to say, we have to rethink financial
literacy and financial capability.

01:10:29.220 --> 01:10:32.270
And, that shift is just beginning to mo,
to, to happen.

01:10:32.270 --> 01:10:37.390
And you're seeing organizations,
companies, research institutes,

01:10:37.390 --> 01:10:39.640
government agencies totally rethinking,

01:10:39.640 --> 01:10:43.970
what do people have to do in order to
prepare differently for retirement.

01:10:43.970 --> 01:10:45.330
And what you'll hear people talk about,

01:10:45.330 --> 01:10:49.000
those of us who are close to this, you
hear us talking about having to

01:10:49.000 --> 01:10:54.070
make our defined contribution plans look a
lot more like pension plans.

01:10:56.100 --> 01:11:00.080
Defaulting in, not requiring people to
make a lot of choices.

01:11:00.080 --> 01:11:04.770
And so that's where you will see the
direction of this, this area of study go.

01:11:05.830 --> 01:11:10.240
The second thing, around working longer,
it's a critical element,

01:11:10.240 --> 01:11:13.210
you have got to work longer, you have got
to earn more.

01:11:13.210 --> 01:11:15.530
One thing that I thought would be
interesting to point out,

01:11:15.530 --> 01:11:19.290
because it's, it's, it's in the news a lot
and

01:11:19.290 --> 01:11:24.630
these, sort of interest generating
headlines about intergenerational warfare.

01:11:24.630 --> 01:11:27.140
Because the boomers, because they're not
retiring, or

01:11:27.140 --> 01:11:30.360
stealing jobs from the millenials.

01:11:30.360 --> 01:11:30.970
Well, Alish,

01:11:30.970 --> 01:11:34.740
Alisha Munnell at Boston College,
basically says that's not the case.

01:11:36.270 --> 01:11:39.220
A study she published about a year ago
indicates that at a macro,

01:11:39.220 --> 01:11:43.740
macroeconomic level there is no, there's
more than enough jobs for everybody.

01:11:43.740 --> 01:11:46.650
I happen to also spend a lot of time with

01:11:46.650 --> 01:11:49.150
a number of HR benefit consulting
companies.

01:11:49.150 --> 01:11:51.310
And they'll tell you that is a very
incorrect and

01:11:51.310 --> 01:11:52.970
controversial statement, at a company
level.

01:11:55.920 --> 01:11:58.340
So, increasing work life, the role of the
employer.

01:11:58.340 --> 01:12:00.260
There are a number of people who think,
well,

01:12:00.260 --> 01:12:01.700
people really don't wanna work longer.

01:12:03.460 --> 01:12:07.590
Bob Clark actually said, well if the
employer encourages it, they want to.

01:12:10.680 --> 01:12:12.110
And third, I have to laugh.

01:12:12.110 --> 01:12:16.110
I was just on the phone with Gopy
Shalgoda,

01:12:16.110 --> 01:12:17.920
who is an author of this third paper.

01:12:17.920 --> 01:12:21.460
I was just talking to her about the study
and this is proof where, you know,

01:12:21.460 --> 01:12:23.440
I come out of industry and I'm sort of
practical and

01:12:23.440 --> 01:12:27.490
translational and Gopy is a unbelievably
awesome researcher.

01:12:27.490 --> 01:12:29.300
And I was sort of asking her for

01:12:29.300 --> 01:12:35.160
clarification on the conclusion I wanted
to throw out about this paper.

01:12:35.160 --> 01:12:40.070
And she said, you can say that, but you
also have to say dah, da, da, da, da.

01:12:40.070 --> 01:12:41.465
I'm like, no, Gopy, I wanna say one
sentence.

01:12:41.465 --> 01:12:41.965
[LAUGH].

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:48.440
And that one sent, sentence is there was a
lot of speculation that with the adoption

01:12:48.440 --> 01:12:52.350
of the Affordable Healthcare Act, you'd
see an uptick in retirement,

01:12:52.350 --> 01:12:56.710
because now people have access to retire
healthcare plans where

01:12:56.710 --> 01:12:59.342
previously they would have had to stay
with their employer, and

01:12:59.342 --> 01:13:05.260
Gopy's caviatic studies is no, that's
actually not the case.

01:13:06.360 --> 01:13:10.310
Access to the Affordable Healthcare Act
within regulated environments is

01:13:10.310 --> 01:13:16.410
not causing people to retire earlier than
they would have.

01:13:16.410 --> 01:13:18.830
So, what are we at the center doing?

01:13:18.830 --> 01:13:22.337
Again, I don't have the luxury of telling
you here's the gadget, here's the widget,

01:13:22.337 --> 01:13:23.100
here's the shoe.

01:13:23.100 --> 01:13:25.210
Here's the, the, the pretty colored stuff.

01:13:25.210 --> 01:13:28.120
We spend a lot of time looking at how do
we

01:13:28.120 --> 01:13:32.220
make these defined contribution plans look
like pension plans.

01:13:32.220 --> 01:13:35.610
How can you include income-generating
products into the plans?

01:13:35.610 --> 01:13:38.750
How can you apply behavioral science, to
the process, so

01:13:38.750 --> 01:13:43.030
you don't require people to make as many
choices and become as involved?

01:13:44.170 --> 01:13:45.570
Knowledgeable!

01:13:45.570 --> 01:13:48.800
But not force them to become expert and

01:13:48.800 --> 01:13:51.890
we're exploring this new idea of financial
wellness,

01:13:51.890 --> 01:13:57.030
employers really are very conscious to the
fact that there are intermediate financial

01:13:57.030 --> 01:14:01.460
decisions that prevent people from
planning for retirement.

01:14:01.460 --> 01:14:04.010
And they understand that if they want to
help their employees plan for

01:14:04.010 --> 01:14:06.180
retirement, they are going to have to help
them plan for

01:14:06.180 --> 01:14:10.600
buying the house, putting a kid through
school, dealing with a student debt.

01:14:10.600 --> 01:14:14.250
So there's this big push starting with
large employers,

01:14:14.250 --> 01:14:17.030
to really look at financial wellness in a
comprehensive way.

01:14:17.030 --> 01:14:20.750
And then in the working longer space,
we've been spending,

01:14:20.750 --> 01:14:23.680
we've spent about the last year and a half
working with employers,

01:14:23.680 --> 01:14:28.145
really trying to understand, well, how do
you adopt to an aging workforce?

01:14:28.145 --> 01:14:29.050
One, do you want to?

01:14:30.050 --> 01:14:31.173
Most of them say, no.

01:14:31.173 --> 01:14:35.990
[LAUGH] But two, they're, they're
beginning to understand they're gonna need

01:14:35.990 --> 01:14:39.670
to, whether it's because of labor force
gaps or whether becau,

01:14:39.670 --> 01:14:41.810
it's because people aren't retiring.

01:14:41.810 --> 01:14:44.110
They're seeing their workforces age.

01:14:45.540 --> 01:14:50.070
So, we're about to tackle a, a project
looking at the, the myths and

01:14:50.070 --> 01:14:55.050
realities of older worker productivity or
multigenerational workforce productivity.

01:14:55.050 --> 01:15:00.010
And then we spend a lot of time in the
area of fraud which if you had

01:15:00.010 --> 01:15:03.080
told me five years ago if I would be as
passionate about as I am today,

01:15:03.080 --> 01:15:04.850
I would have said, what?

01:15:04.850 --> 01:15:10.550
But the problem of fraud and the amount of
money we lose to fraud is enormous.

01:15:10.550 --> 01:15:13.760
And frankly puts an older population at

01:15:13.760 --> 01:15:16.230
greater risk cuz they can't recover quite
as rapidly.

01:15:17.580 --> 01:15:20.770
So we have ac, actually been working quite
diligently trying to

01:15:20.770 --> 01:15:24.370
catalyze more interest in researching
issues of fraud.

01:15:24.370 --> 01:15:27.300
And one of the things that we discovered
is that there is

01:15:27.300 --> 01:15:31.490
actually no good standard classification
of fraud so you're talking to one

01:15:31.490 --> 01:15:35.550
government agency who's sponsoring fraud
prevention efforts and

01:15:35.550 --> 01:15:40.410
they're defining fraud very differently
than the agency down the block.

01:15:40.410 --> 01:15:42.080
And why is that relevant?

01:15:42.080 --> 01:15:44.540
It's because money chases the definition.

01:15:44.540 --> 01:15:49.660
So, we think we can actually help get more
money focused on solving the problems of

01:15:49.660 --> 01:15:52.830
fraud detection and prevention if we could
get standard classification and

01:15:52.830 --> 01:15:54.020
a standard measure of fraud.

01:15:57.180 --> 01:16:01.750
So, we're not the only ones looking at
preparing for retirement differently.

01:16:01.750 --> 01:16:07.480
There is a proliferation of companies that
are looking at banking differently,

01:16:07.480 --> 01:16:10.080
investing differently, spending
differently.

01:16:11.620 --> 01:16:14.450
Helping you from the minute you get your
first job all the way to

01:16:14.450 --> 01:16:16.270
the time you retire.

01:16:16.270 --> 01:16:18.040
Government agencies are at play.

01:16:18.040 --> 01:16:20.880
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau
just issued

01:16:20.880 --> 01:16:23.190
a brief on financial wellness at work.

01:16:23.190 --> 01:16:27.307
This company called Finan, Finan,
Financial Finesse is a company that is

01:16:27.307 --> 01:16:31.241
actually going in and implementing
financial wellness programs, and

01:16:31.241 --> 01:16:33.585
companies are doing it quite successfully.

01:16:33.585 --> 01:16:38.081
So, the world has shifted and the world is
about to shift again and

01:16:38.081 --> 01:16:43.128
I wish I could give you different news
than we need to work longer and save more.

01:16:43.128 --> 01:16:45.636
But, I can tell you one, the working
longer thing,

01:16:45.636 --> 01:16:48.867
there are many other positive outcomes for
working longer.

01:16:48.867 --> 01:16:52.204
And I think we can crack the nut on how to
save more in

01:16:52.204 --> 01:16:56.909
a way which doesn't make it sound like I'm
saying eat your broccoli.

01:16:56.909 --> 01:17:01.483
[APPLAUSE].

01:17:01.483 --> 01:17:11.483
[MUSIC]

